<script>
  import BlocksDemo from '$lib/demos/BlocksDemo.svelte';
</script>

Some pages are not written by developers at all. A marketing team assembles a landing page in a visual editor, or a CMS decides which components appear on a page and in what order, and your app only has to render what it is given. When a developer writes the layout in a `.svelte` file you don't need any of this, because the components alone are enough.

For the cases where content decides the layout, a **block** page is data: a tree of nodes in which each node names a component `type` and its `props`. `blocks()` renders that tree the same way `markdown()` renders `.svx`, as an entry `body` that you place with `<Region of={entry.body} />`. It is the rendering half of a visual page builder: your CMS (or a JSON file) owns the layout, and ogygia owns the rendering.

The panel below is a block tree (a `Hero`, a `Grid` of two `Feature`s, and one interactive `CounterBlock`) rendered live from the data shown further down this page.

<div class="doc-demo">
  <BlocksDemo />
</div>

## Register your blocks

The registry maps a block `type` name to a component. A **static** block is a `with { region: 'raw' }` import, which renders inline and ships no JS but is still code-split, so a page loads the CSS (and, for interactive blocks, the JS) of only the block types it names. An **interactive** block uses `with { wake: … }` instead, which bakes in its schedule (`load`, `idle`, `visible` or a media query decides when it hydrates) and makes its JS **load only when a page names it**.

```ts title=src/lib/blocks.ts
// a .ts module: a `region`/`wake` mark here makes a held block
import Hero    from './blocks/Hero.svelte'    with { region: 'raw' };
import Grid    from './blocks/Grid.svelte'    with { region: 'raw' };
import Feature from './blocks/Feature.svelte' with { region: 'raw' };
import Counter from './blocks/Counter.svelte' with { wake: 'load' };

export const registry = { Hero, Grid, Feature, Counter };
```

Both marks make the component a [held region](/docs/regions/held-regions), code-split and resolved to a region by the registry; `region: 'raw'` bakes in no schedule (static, HTML only) and `wake:` bakes one in (interactive). A plain import would also work, but its CSS would be folded into the page stylesheet, so a large registry would cost every page the styles of every block. The marks are what keep a page's cost limited to the blocks it renders.

> Writing one `with { region: 'raw' }` line per block means N imports that quietly drift out of sync with the folder. The **[`regions()` macro](/docs/macros/regions)** collapses them into one line, `import.meta.og.regions('./blocks/*.svelte')`, which globs the folder and emits a raw-region import for each match, keyed by basename. Blocks that need a `wake:` schedule stay as manual imports spread on top.

You decide which component each `type` means; there is no guessing and no auto-discovery. An unregistered `type` is skipped with a dev warning and is never rendered as something it is not.

## A page is JSON

```json
[
  { "type": "Hero", "props": { "title": "Welcome" } },
  { "type": "Grid", "children": [
    { "type": "Feature", "props": { "title": "Fast" } },
    { "type": "Feature", "props": { "title": "Typed" } }
  ] },
  { "type": "Counter", "props": { "start": 3 } }
]
```

Nodes nest through `children`, so a `Grid` can hold `Feature`s and a section can hold a grid. That nesting is what makes the data a page tree rather than a flat stack.

## Render it

`blocks()` is a content source. Point a collection at your page JSON, and each entry's `body` is the rendered tree:

```ts
import { content, blocks } from '@ogygia/content';
import { registry } from '$lib/blocks';

export const pages = content({
  loader: blocks(import.meta.glob('./pages/*.json'), registry)
});
```

You then render it exactly like a markdown body, with the same `get()` and the same `<Region>`. Call `get()` in a **universal** `+page.ts` load, or directly in the page component with `await`, so that its data reaches the render by reference within the same server pass.

```ts title=+page.ts
// universal, NOT +page.server.ts
import { pages } from '$lib/blocks/pages';
export const load = async () => ({ page: await pages.get('landing') });
```

```svelte
<script>
  import { Region } from 'ogygia';
  let { data } = $props();
</script>

<Region of={data.page.body} />
```

::: warning Not `+page.server.ts`
A server load's data crosses a serialization boundary, and a `body` is a same-pass SSR value (a live render, not wire data), so it cannot cross. ogygia stops you with an error that says so. On a `csr = false` page a universal load never runs on the client, so it is server code without that boundary.
:::

The **CSS of each block travels with its render**. The page never imports `Hero` or `Feature`, so their styles are not in any page stylesheet. Instead, during the server pass the region links each rendered block's hashed stylesheet into `<head>`, deduplicated and only for the block types the page uses, so a registry of 100 blocks costs a page exactly the stylesheets of the blocks it renders.

## No collection? A ten-line recipe

The content source is the recommended path, but when you already have a tree in hand (a prop, a literal, a one-off CMS call), you don't need a collection. `blocks.resolve(tree, registry)` turns the tree into region nodes. It must run **on the server**, where the signing key lives, which means a `+page.server.ts` load, a remote, or the SSR pass of a `csr = false` page. You render the nodes with a small recomposer that you own:

```svelte title=BlockTree.svelte
<!-- yours to keep and tweak -->
<script>
  import { Region } from 'ogygia';
  import { blocks } from '@ogygia/content';
  import Self from './BlockTree.svelte';

  let { tree, registry, nodes } = $props();
  const resolved = $derived(nodes ?? blocks.resolve(tree, registry));
</script>

{#each resolved as node, i (i)}
  <Region of={node.of}>{#if node.children?.length}<Self nodes={node.children} />{/if}</Region>
{/each}
```

```svelte
<!-- use it -->
<BlockTree tree={page} {registry} />
```

That is all there is to it: `blocks.resolve` walks the tree from `type` to region and skips unknown types, and your component does the rendering. It is exactly what the `blocks()` source runs internally. ogygia ships no component for this because ten lines that you own are easier to adapt than a component you can't see into.

## Data from a CMS (Builder.io)

Builder is headless, meaning it serves your pages as JSON over an HTTP API. Write a **loader** that fetches them and render through `blocks()`. With your public key filled in, the following is a working Builder-driven site:

```ts title=src/lib/builder.ts
import { content, blocks, mapRaw } from '@ogygia/content';
import type { RawSource } from '@ogygia/content';
import { registry } from './blocks';

const KEY = 'YOUR_BUILDER_PUBLIC_KEY';
const API = `https://cdn.builder.io/api/v3/content/page?apiKey=${KEY}`;

// Builder element → block node.
const toTree = (els = []) =>
  els.filter((e) => e.component?.name).map((e) => ({
    type: e.component.name,
    props: e.component.options ?? {},
    children: e.children ? toTree(e.children) : undefined
  }));

// A raw source over Builder's Content API: refs() lists every page, get() fetches ONE.
const builderApi: RawSource<any> = {
  async refs() {
    const { results } = await fetch(`${API}&limit=100`).then((r) => r.json());
    return results.map((r) => ({ id: (r.data?.url ?? r.id).replace(/^\//, ''), value: r }));
  },
  async get(id) {
    const { results } = await fetch(`${API}&limit=1&url=/${id}`).then((r) => r.json());
    return results[0] ? { id, value: results[0] } : null;
  }
};

// Convert each result to a block tree, then render through blocks().
export const pages = content({
  loader: blocks(mapRaw(builderApi, (r) => ({ blocks: toTree(r.data?.blocks) })), registry)
});
```

That is the whole integration. `get(slug)` fetches one page on demand, `list()` / `ids()` drive navigation or prerendering, and nothing else in your app changes, because it is a content collection like any other.

The live demo on this site uses local JSON in exactly Builder's shape: **[/demo/builder/home](/demo/builder/home)** and **[/demo/builder/pricing](/demo/builder/pricing)** are full pages assembled as data and rendered as islands, and only the interactive block ships JS.

## Thousands of blocks, only a few shipped

The registry can hold a thousand blocks while a page that names three loads three chunks, and the other 997 never ship. This is not a special case but a consequence of the model: the registry (the vocabulary) is fixed at build time, the page JSON picks the arrangement at runtime, and each block is a held region whose client chunk loads only when it is on the page. The bundle therefore stays small however large the registry grows; [Held regions](/docs/regions/held-regions) covers the mechanism.
